WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:09.100
Right now, corporations are racing to integrate
artificial intelligence into daily operations.

2
00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:13.920
65% of companies plan major investments
to drive efficiency this year.

3
00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:17.060
Look at this Computas data on the AI paradox.

4
00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:21.320
Only 8% of companies measured concrete value
from AI investments.

5
00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:24.640
Why? 9 out of 10 cite lack of competence.

6
00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:27.840
It's not software. Only 3% lack tools.

7
00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:31.120
The friction now rests entirely on human factors:

8
00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:33.880
uncertainty, competence, and legitimacy.

9
00:00:34.580 --> 00:00:39.080
Now, looking at this next breakdown, you can see
the friction isn't at the individual level.

10
00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:40.520
Employees are ready.

11
00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:46.300
Implementation fails because the organizational systems
and leadership practices built around them stand in the way.

12
00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:52.200
Corporate implementation stalls because of a systemic
leadership and management gap, not a software failure.

13
00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:57.820
Inside modern organizations, this management gap
creates two isolated islands of operation.

14
00:00:58.380 --> 00:00:59.900
This diagram maps the divide.

15
00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:02.800
On the left, you have the technical department:

16
00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:07.760
teams focused on coding, cloud infrastructure,
and training machine learning models.

17
00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:10.520
On the right side, you have the business leadership.

18
00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:15.320
They focus on strategy, return on investment,
and key performance indicators.

19
00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:18.380
Because these two factions speak different languages,

20
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:22.340
technical models frequently fail
to solve actual business problems.

21
00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:25.560
The solution requires a specific kind of professional.

22
00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:29.300
Look at the center of the graphic:
the analytics translator.

23
00:01:29.980 --> 00:01:33.780
This hybrid leader builds a bridge between
the technical and business cliffs.

24
00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:38.160
Without this translator, a company has
computing power, but no direction.

25
00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:42.080
With one, they can turn implementation
into measurable profit.

26
00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:46.420
Traditionally, data science has relied
on a model-first approach.

27
00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:52.140
Developers take data, build an algorithm, and then
look for a useful result at the end of the pipeline.

28
00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:55.020
Decision intelligence reverses this workflow.

29
00:01:55.580 --> 00:02:00.460
In this framework, the process begins on the left
with the desired effect, the value

30
00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:03.420
or ROI, and the specific business decision.

31
00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:06.720
Once the goal is locked in, the workflow moves backward.

32
00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:12.360
You determine the required analysis and only then
source the raw data needed to achieve it.

33
00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:17.640
This discipline ensures that teams never waste
resources building a complex model

34
00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:20.780
without a clear purpose and a business outcome
already in place.

35
00:02:21.580 --> 00:02:26.160
Decision intelligence shifts the corporate focus
away from simply acquiring technology

36
00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:29.240
and towards steering a business
with informed precision.

37
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:34.580
To train this new class of bridge builders,
the University of South-Eastern Norway in Drammen

38
00:02:34.580 --> 00:02:38.020
developed the master's program in
strategic data science and management.

39
00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:41.780
This course overview shows how they develop
that hybrid skill set.

40
00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:46.120
The curriculum blends advanced data platforms
and AI with economics,

41
00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:48.600
change management, and strategic sustainability.

42
00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:52.080
This specific mix targets current market requirements.

43
00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:58.380
Looking at this needs analysis, integrating AI
into decisions and managing through change

44
00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:01.540
sit right at the top of the priority list
for industry partners.

45
00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:08.540
Students also dive into responsible AI, learning
to navigate the privacy, security, and legal

46
00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:11.500
frameworks that typically stall
unprepared organizations.

47
00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:17.820
These leaders understand the underlying code and
the financial realities required to make it profitable.

48
00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:23.240
This training opens up leadership paths for graduates,
qualifying them for roles like

49
00:03:23.240 --> 00:03:28.960
analytics lead, AI product owner, and strategic
advisor in both the public and private sectors.

50
00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:35.500
While generative AI handles routine coding tasks,
the human translator provides the strategic

51
00:03:35.500 --> 00:03:42.680
synthesis, connecting technical outputs to specific business
targets, that currently requires human context and judgment.

52
00:03:43.540 --> 00:03:46.360
The market is already saturated with raw technology.

53
00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:50.260
What organizations need are the leaders
who know how to manage it.

54
00:03:50.780 --> 00:03:55.580
Mastering strategic data science provides the hybrid
skill set that allows organizations

55
00:03:55.580 --> 00:03:59.020
to bridge the gap between technical potential
and the bottom line.
